1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:03,270
It's 4 o'clock in the morning on a Thursday,

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:08,500
and the night shift on Line 3 is doing its 2-hourly seal check.

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:14,750
The operator takes 3 packs off the end of the line and drops them in the leak tester,

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,600
and 2 of them leak along the seal.

5
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:27,280
That means everything we've packed since the last good check, about 6,000 packs, goes on hold.

6
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:36,020
Then the phones start ringing: first the production manager's at home, then the QA manager's.

7
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:44,360
A retailer already complained about slack packs last month, and this is the 6th time this year.

8
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:49,420
When I ran the operation, this was the call nobody wanted,

9
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:54,350
because I knew the general manager would ask two questions.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:01,840
Why does this keep happening, and what is it actually costing us?

11
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,760
So what do we want?

12
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,380
First, find the real cause, not just the faulty pack.

13
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:17,260
Second, understand the financial impact to the business.

14
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,420
Third, put in a countermeasure that holds.

15
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,980
And finally, prove it, months later.

16
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:31,760
I'll do it in RCA Pilot, starting from a blank page.

17
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:36,960
The numbers are illustrative, made up to show the method.

18
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:41,190
We have 6 events a year, and each one stops the line for 8 hours.

19
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:50,490
The line runs at 3,000 packs an hour, with 45 cents margin a pack.

20
00:01:50,540 --> 00:02:00,160
On top of that, each event costs on average about $33,100 in cash.

21
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:09,010
So where do we start?

22
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:14,020
com and make an account with an email and password.

23
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:15,360
You need an account for the AI buttons.

24
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:23,280
I've prepared one earlier, so I'll start with New investigation.

25
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:27,000
Step 1 is the Problem.

26
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,960
This is where a lot of investigations go wrong.

27
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:36,620
Somebody writes "seals bad", and everyone moves on.

28
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:40,500
First I set the Type of failure.

29
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:45,420
A leaking pack is a Process or quality failure.

30
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:52,150
Now, nobody writes a perfect problem statement at 4 o'clock in the morning.

31
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:01,500
A supervisor just jots rough notes, and that's what Draft problem statement from notes is for.

32
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,580
The AI turns the notes into a clear statement, using only what I wrote.

33
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:11,800
It doesn't guess a cause.

34
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:19,110
It also lists what's missing, under Not in your notes, worth finding out.

35
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:22,060
That's your first round of questions.

36
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:27,880
Then I press Use this draft and correct anything that's wrong.

37
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,820
Two small things matter later.

38
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:39,240
If this started as a non-conformance, put the number in Quality reference.

39
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:43,340
Ours is NCR-2291.

40
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:50,090
That way the analysis can feed into your QA and CAPA system.

41
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:57,740
Then fill in the Line / process details, so you can find this investigation again.

42
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:01,520
Step 2 is the Loss.

43
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:05,220
This is the one the general manager cares about.

44
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:11,270
If you don't have figures yet, choose Not known yet, or put in a rough yearly estimate.

45
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:16,160
A rough number beats none.

46
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:23,530
Here we have 6 events a year, 8 hours each, at 3,000 packs an hour.

47
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:28,920
So each event, we don't make 24,000 packs.

48
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:36,420
At 45 cents a pack, that's $10,800 of lost margin.

49
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:38,140
Then there's the cash.

50
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:47,640
There's the $1,200 call-out, and then everything that quietly adds up under Other costs.

51
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:51,010
Scrapped product is $14,500.

52
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:55,640
Product on hold is $3,200.

53
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:57,300
Customer claims and credits are $6,800, retailer penalties $5,000, and rework $2,400.

54
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:14,480
Claims and penalties don't hit every event, so these are averages across the year.

55
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:19,750
That makes $33,100 of cash.

56
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:33,740
So on average, one event costs $43,900, and 6 a year comes to $263,400.

57
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:41,060
And because the line runs at capacity, that output is gone for good.

58
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:45,040
Step 3 is Evidence.

59
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:50,700
In any plant meeting, that means evidence before opinions,

60
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:55,100
because everyone has a theory about those seals.

61
00:05:55,580 --> 00:06:01,520
Each piece gets a title, a type, a date and a note.

62
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:09,740
Here we have a photo of a leaking pack, the sealing temperature log, the jaw inspection,

63
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:17,160
the settings and changeover record, the film specification, and the interviews.

64
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:25,620
Keep the retained packs and the worn parts, and photograph them before anything gets cleaned up.

65
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:32,580
Now we get to Step 4, the Cause tree.

66
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,060
This is where the real thinking happens.

67
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:41,100
The top box is the failure event, and we keep asking Why?

68
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,180
in plain words.

69
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:46,320
Why were the packs leaking?

70
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,980
Because the seals were weak along the flange.

71
00:06:50,860 --> 00:06:51,560
Why?

72
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:58,100
Because the jaw ran 17 degrees low, and the silicone was worn.

73
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:00,600
That's the physical cause.

74
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:05,460
If we stopped there, we'd be back here in a few weeks.

75
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:09,060
So we ask why it wasn't caught.

76
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:16,120
Nothing in the maintenance plan checks the jaw temperature or replaces that silicone.

77
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:25,160
That's the system level, and the real root, so I mark it with This is a root cause.

78
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:29,380
The other branches work the same way.

79
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:34,780
Operators raised the speed and cut the dwell time to keep up.

80
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,190
A new film batch came in.

81
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:41,150
And the seal check was only 3 packs every 2 hours.

82
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:48,920
For each cause, I tick the evidence and mark it Verified or Ruled out.

83
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:53,040
If I can't prove it, it stays a theory.

84
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:59,120
With equipment, I always ask one question.

85
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:02,240
Is the wear natural, or forced?

86
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:06,760
Silicone on a sealing jaw wears out eventually.

87
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,540
That's natural.

88
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:17,000
But running it a year past its life, because nothing prompted anyone to change it, is forced.

89
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:22,810
That's wear we allowed, so I record it under Type of wear.

90
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:31,260
The same tree also shows as 5 Whys, or as a Fishbone grouped by Cause area.

91
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:34,460
Now the AI help.

92
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:39,380
I treat it as a second pair of eyes, not the boss.

93
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:44,440
The Cause coach suggests causes I may have missed.

94
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:52,600
Each one says what would confirm it or rule it out, and I choose Add to tree or Dismiss.

95
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:56,880
It suggests product contaminating the flange.

96
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:00,250
I hadn't thought of that, so it goes in.

97
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:09,420
It also suggests the gas mix, but the oxygen readings were in spec, so I dismiss it.

98
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:13,200
Next, I click Challenge my analysis.

99
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:18,620
It reviews the tree the way an experienced investigator would.

100
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:23,980
It points out that the film root cause has no evidence linked.

101
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:28,030
That's fair, so I link the film inspection.

102
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:30,270
The AI suggests.

103
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:32,040
You decide.

104
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:36,440
Step 5 is Actions.

105
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:42,740
This is where a lot of RCAs quietly die, in a list of "we should".

106
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:48,800
So every action gets an owner, a due date and a cost.

107
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:52,460
Corrective actions deal with this failure.

108
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:56,600
Preventive actions stop it coming back.

109
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:02,870
For the jaw, it's a Maintenance task: check the temperature every week,

110
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:06,360
and replace the silicone every 3 months.

111
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:14,100
For the settings, it's a Design or equipment change, to lock them by recipe.

112
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:20,780
For the film, it's a Procedure or standard, with supplier change control.

113
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:26,150
And for the seal checks, it's more frequent checks and a faster leak tester.

114
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:31,600
The total cost is $20,200.

115
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:39,820
If you get stuck for ideas, each root cause has a Suggested actions button.

116
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:43,980
Nothing goes in until I click Add to actions.

117
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:51,460
And the Quality check lists any gaps, like a root cause with no action against it.

118
00:10:54,800 --> 00:11:02,140
Now the part almost nobody does: Step 6, proving it stopped.

119
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,660
Savings only count once every action is Done,

120
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,100
the review date is in, and the failure hasn't come back.

121
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:15,860
So, months later, I mark the actions Done.

122
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:20,870
I stay cautious and expect 1 event a year, not zero.

123
00:11:20,920 --> 00:11:39,570
That brings the loss down to $43,900 a year, so the verified saving is $219,500.

124
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:44,460
1 months, and we got 40 hours of line time back.

125
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:48,770
Then I ask where else this could happen.

126
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:53,460
With Roll out this countermeasure,

127
00:11:54,180 --> 00:12:00,000
I add a check for Lines 1 and 2, each with an owner and a date.

128
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:04,120
The last step is the Report.

129
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:07,820
This is where you answer the general manager's two questions.

130
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:13,900
I click Draft report, and it writes the report from everything I've entered.

131
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:19,340
The Management summary is the one page the general manager will actually read.

132
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:23,710
I check every fact, because my name goes on it.

133
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:26,500
Then I Print or Save as PDF.

134
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:32,960
So let's go back to that Thursday morning, and that meeting.

135
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:36,120
Why does this keep happening?

136
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:41,920
Because nothing in our system checked the jaw, locked the settings,

137
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:46,060
controlled the film, or caught a bad seal early.

138
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:48,920
It was never one unlucky operator.

139
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:50,640
What is it costing us?

140
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:56,130
$263,400 a year.

141
00:12:56,180 --> 00:13:01,690
And the proof the countermeasure works is $219,

142
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:09,460
500 saved and verified, and 40 hours of line time back.

143
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:12,920
And this is the dashboard.

144
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:16,720
You see it all on one screen: what failures are costing you,

145
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:26,020
what you've saved and verified, and the actions still open.

146
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:33,350
That's the method.

147
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:34,270
com.

148
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:37,080
The link is in the description.

